.TH NESTEST 1
.SH NAME
nestest \- check nes(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nestest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nestest
checks
.IR nes (2)
itself, which the games built on it only exercise indirectly:
each of those asserts on its own game state and would pass with every
tile drawn in the wrong palette.
This one draws into memory and reads the pixels back, comparing them
against the palette entries they are supposed to be, so it can see what
a game's test cannot.
Nothing here needs a window.
.SS What it checks
.TP
the master palette
That the greys are grey and climb from dark to white, that column 13 is
black, that no two of the twelve hues on a row come out the same colour,
that a hue brightens down the rows, and that
.I entry
clamps rather than faulting on a row or column out of range.
.TP
tile sheets
That art written as strings decodes to the colour indices it reads as,
that
.I meta
puts the four quadrants of a 16 by 16 in the order top left, top right,
bottom left, bottom right \- getting that wrong scrambles every
character in every game \- and that the font loads with 256 glyphs, a
non-blank
.B A
and a blank space.
.TP
drawing
That a surface's image is the scale times its logical size and starts in
its background colour;
that
.I tile
paints the palette's colours at tile coordinates and that a logical
pixel really is a block of real ones;
that the composite cache is keyed by palette as well as by tile, so the
same tile drawn in a second palette comes out in the second palette and
then back again in the first;
that
.I put
leaves colour 0 alone while
.I tile
paints it as the background;
that
.B FLIPX
and
.B FLIPY
move what they should;
that
.I put16
lands its quadrants where
.I meta
put them and that
.B FLIPX
swaps the halves as well as mirroring each;
and that
.I text
puts one glyph every eight logical pixels and draws nothing for a space.
.TP
view and blit
That
.I view
shows the window of a level it is asked for, and that it clamps at both
ends \- past the end it must show the last screen, not a repeat of the
level, which is what
.I draw
would do on its own with a source shorter than its destination.
.TP
the pad
That a press sets its bit and a release clears it, for the arrows and
the letters alike, that
.I edge
reports only what is new, and that a key the pad does not use changes
nothing rather than clearing it.
.PP
The last line is
.B PASSED
or
.BR FAILED .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/nestest.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/nestest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nes (2),
.IR rivet (1),
.IR sprocket (1),
.IR starling (1),
.IR orchard (1)
.SH BUGS
It checks that the palette's entries differ from each other, not that
any of them is the right colour: there is no reference to be right
against, the ramp being computed.
A change to the constants at the top of
.B nes.b
that made every colour uglier would pass.
.PP
It says nothing about how a game looks.
Each game's
.B -s
writes a frame out to be looked at, and that check is a manual one.
